  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "S \\text{ symmetric} \\iff a \\in \\mathbb{Z}\\setminus S \\implies (g - a) \\in S, \\quad g = F(S),",
    "plain": "A numerical semigroup $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$ (cofinite, closed under addition, containing $0$) is *symmetric* when its set of gaps is exactly the \"mirror image\" of $S$ across the Frobenius number $g$: for every integer $a$, exactly one of $a$, $g-a$ lies in $S$. For two generators this is automatic; the open question is to characterize symmetry for semigroups with three or more generators using Apéry sets (Kunz, 1970), and to determine which generator tuples preserve the involution $k \\mapsto g - k$.",
